--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertCompletes` refuses where no `#[Test(at: ...)]` fixed a clock, says so, and spends nothing -- `rule:testing/task-tree-and-virtual-clock`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The budget is spent against the clock `#[Test(at: ...)]` fixed, so the
// member's boundary is exactly "there is no clock to spend it against". A
// `.nvst` case is top-level statements and is therefore never inside a
// `#[Test]`, which makes this file the one place that boundary is observable
// from the language.
//
// The accepted side of the same bound is `crates/nvs-cli`'s
// `assert_completes_passes_a_body_that_finishes_under_the_clock`, which needs a
// runner to observe and so cannot be asked here.

use Core\Test;
use Core\Time;

// 1. What the refusal says. A call written where there is no clock is a bug at
// the call site, so it is `LogicError` -- the same class `Core\Test::advance`
// refuses with, because it is the same missing clock.
try {
    Core\Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => { echo "the budget was spent\n"; }, {within: 50ms});
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// 2. The same refusal through the member a test would reach for, by class and
// by ancestor -- written out in full and through the `use` above, which are one
// member either way.
Core\Test::assertThrows(
    fn (): void => { Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => {}, {within: 50ms}); },
    LogicError::class,
);
Test::assertThrows(
    fn (): void => { Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => {}, {within: 50ms}); },
    Throwable::class,
);
echo "assertCompletes without a fixed clock is refused\n";

// 3. The refusal spent nothing. A member that quietly advanced *something* --
// the host's clock, a process-wide offset -- would still pass parts 1 and 2
// while breaking, which is why the bound is asserted on both sides rather than
// by the throw alone.
int $before = Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
Test::assertThrows(
    fn (): void => { Test::assertCompletes(fn (): void => {}, {within: 4000d}); },
    LogicError::class,
);
int $after = Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
Test::assertTrue($after - $before < 60);
echo "the host clock is unmoved\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertCompletes(): this test declared no `at:`, so it has no fixed clock to spend `within` against
assertCompletes without a fixed clock is refused
the host clock is unmoved
